  <!-- ARTICLE CONTENT — EDIT: replace all copy + images below -->
  <article class="article-content">

    <section id="what-are">
      <h2>What Is Laser Treatment for Varicose Veins?</h2>
      <p>Laser treatment, also known as Endovenous Laser Ablation (EVLA) or Endovenous Laser Treatment (EVLT), is a minimally invasive procedure used to close off damaged veins that cause varicose veins. Instead of cutting and removing the vein through open surgery, a thin laser fiber is inserted directly into the affected vein. The laser energy heats the vein wall from the inside, causing it to collapse and seal shut.</p>
      <p>Once the faulty vein is closed, blood naturally reroutes through healthy veins nearby, and the body gradually absorbs the treated vein over the following months. This approach has largely replaced traditional vein stripping surgery in modern vascular care, since it achieves similar or better results with far less trauma to the leg.</p>

      <div class="inline-image">
        <!-- REPLACE: supporting image -->
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1631815589968-fdb09a223b1e?w=900&q=80" alt="Endovenous laser ablation procedure illustration for varicose veins">
        <figcaption>Endovenous laser ablation seals the faulty vein from the inside using targeted heat energy</figcaption>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="symptoms">
      <h2>How the Procedure Works</h2>
      <p>Laser treatment for varicose veins is typically completed in under an hour and is performed under local anesthesia, meaning you stay awake throughout. Here's what the process generally involves:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An ultrasound scan maps the damaged vein and confirms it's suitable for laser closure</li>
        <li>Local anesthesia numbs the treatment area along the vein</li>
        <li>A thin laser fiber is guided into the vein through a small needle puncture — no large incisions are needed</li>
        <li>Laser energy is delivered as the fiber is slowly withdrawn, sealing the vein along its length</li>
        <li>A compression bandage or stocking is applied immediately after the procedure</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>No hospital stay required:</strong> Because the procedure is minimally invasive, most patients walk out the same day and resume light activity within 24 to 48 hours.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="causes">
      <h2>Key Benefits of Laser Treatment</h2>
      <p>Compared to traditional vein stripping surgery, laser treatment offers a number of advantages that make it the preferred choice for most patients with varicose veins today.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Minimally Invasive</h4>
          <p>No large incisions — just a single needle puncture, which means less tissue damage and a lower risk of infection.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Minimal Pain</h4>
          <p>Performed under local anesthesia with little to no pain during or after the procedure, unlike surgical stripping.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Faster Recovery</h4>
          <p>Most patients return to normal daily activities within 1-2 days, compared to 1-2 weeks after open surgery.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>No General Anesthesia</h4>
          <p>The procedure is done under local anesthesia, removing the risks associated with going under general anesthesia.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Better Cosmetic Outcome</h4>
          <p>Since there are no surgical cuts, there's virtually no visible scarring once the leg has healed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>High Success Rate</h4>
          <p>Clinical studies consistently show success rates above 95% in closing the treated vein permanently.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MID-ARTICLE CTA -->
    <div class="mid-cta">
      <div>
        <h3>Wondering if laser treatment is right for you?</h3>
        <p>Get a free vein health screening at Medfine, BTM Layout.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="#" class="btn-primary">Book Free Screening →</a>
    </div>

    <section id="treatment">
      <h2>Recovery &amp; Aftercare</h2>
      <p>One of the biggest advantages of laser treatment is how quickly patients bounce back. Recovery is typically broken down into three phases.</p>

      <h3>1. Immediate Aftercare (Day 1-2)</h3>
      <p>You'll be encouraged to walk shortly after the procedure, as movement helps prevent blood clots and supports circulation. A compression stocking is usually worn continuously for the first few days to support the leg and reduce swelling.</p>

      <h3>2. First Two Weeks</h3>
      <p>Mild bruising, tightness, or a pulling sensation along the treated vein is normal and resolves on its own. Most patients return to desk-based work within a day or two, while strenuous exercise and heavy lifting are usually avoided for about two weeks.</p>

      <div class="inline-image">
        <!-- REPLACE: procedure/clinic image -->
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1582719478250-c89cae4dc85b?w=900&q=80" alt="Patient recovery after laser treatment for varicose veins">
        <figcaption>Most patients resume light daily activity within 24 to 48 hours of treatment</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>3. Long-Term Results</h3>
      <p>The treated vein is gradually reabsorbed by the body over the following months, and visible improvement in the appearance of the leg continues during this time. Follow-up ultrasound scans are typically scheduled to confirm the vein has closed successfully.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- AUTHOR BIO -->
    <div class="author-bio">
      <!-- REPLACE: author photo -->
      <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1582750433449-648ed127bb54?w=200&q=80" alt="Doctor headshot">
      <div>
        <div class="label">Reviewed By</div>
        <h4>Dr. [Author Name], [Qualification]</h4>
        <p>[One-line credibility note — years of experience, specialization, hospital affiliation.]</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
